\title{Quantile Advice and CVaR Optimization:
Retained Results and a Tail-Localized Rank Frontier}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{The pair and the connexion pursued}

Q, \emph{Learning-Augmented Algorithms: Guarantees, Construction
Mechanisms, and System-Level Implications}, distinguishes prediction
interfaces, charged prediction access, and achieved versus matched
guarantees. P, \emph{Distributed risk-averse optimization via CVaR},
studies distributed zeroth-order optimization from independent noisy
loss evaluations, proving consensus and convergence to an expected
empirical smoothed surrogate.

The connexion pursued was whether predicting a value-at-risk threshold
could reduce P's expensive loss queries. P's oracle does not identify
tail scenarios before their losses are evaluated. A predicted threshold
therefore cannot supply cheap screening without changing the
information interface \ledger{1, 3, 9}.

The preceding consolidation retained an equal-quantile lower bound,
a sharper analysis of P, a convexity obstruction, costed screening,
lifted correction, multilevel rank estimators, robust quadrature,
and a separation between exact and untrusted rankings.
These results remain below. Review 42 identified the missing
noisy-value convex-optimization baseline; the fifth round corrected
it and supplied direct optimization lower bounds
\ledger{42, 44, 47--50}.

The latest review is entry 52. It returned ITERATE, assessed the
preceding account at \([rating withheld]\), and reported that its checks of the
search lemma, hidden-jump construction, rare-bin coupling, pilot
bounds, and surrogate-error bound held. It requested preservation of
those results and proposed a final investigation of the dependence
on tail probability and prediction error \ledger{52}.

The appended sixth round supplies that investigation. In the declared
single-agent rank interface, it strengthens the robust costly-query
bound to
\[
 \widetilde O\!\left(
 \frac{U}{\alpha\epsilon}
 +\frac{\min\{\eta,U\}^2}{\alpha\epsilon^2}
 \right),
\]
with a matching lower expression up to logarithms for algorithms
required to remain correct under arbitrary advice. Here
\(\eta\) bounds the uniform error of scores inducing the ranks;
the algorithm does not know \(\eta\). Draws also improve to
\(\widetilde O(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2))\).
No later verifier review certifies this extension
\ledger{53--61}.

\section{Retained first-round results}

\subsection{Exact quantiles leave tail-mean uncertainty}

\begin{claim}
Under P's unconditional independent loss-sampling interface, exact
knowledge of the quantile at every decision leaves a worst-case
\(\Omega(b^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2))\) query requirement for expected
additive CVaR optimization error \(\epsilon\).
\end{claim}

The construction is in
\url{ada/quantile-obstruction-and-baseline.md},
lines 11--74 \ledger{3, 7}.
For \(x\in[-1,1]\), \(a>b>0\), and
\(0<\gamma\leq1/2\), let
\[
 B\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(\alpha),
 \qquad
 \Pr_v(S=1)=\frac{1+v\gamma}{2},
 \qquad v\in\{-1,+1\},
\]
independently, and return
\[
 J_v(x;B,S)=B(a+bSx).
\]
Every sample loss is affine, \(b\)-Lipschitz, and bounded. Under the
lower-quantile convention,
\[
 q_v(x)=
 \inf\{t:\Pr_v(J_v(x)\leq t)\geq1-\alpha\}=0
\]
for every decision and both instances. Nevertheless,
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 C_v(x)&=a+vb\gamma x,\\
 x_v^\ast&=-v,\\
 C_v(x)-C_v^\ast&=b\gamma(1+vx).
 \end{aligned}
\]
Zero is a minimizing Rockafellar--Uryasev threshold; the support gap
makes that threshold nonunique.

Even granting the stronger oracle that reveals \(B\) and, when
\(B=1\), \(S\), the divergence per sample is
\[
 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_+\Vert P_-)
 =\alpha\gamma\log\frac{1+\gamma}{1-\gamma}
 \leq4\alpha\gamma^2.
\]
Adaptive decisions cannot increase the information in a sample.
For \(n\) queries,
\[
 \operatorname{TV}(P_+^{(n)},P_-^{(n)})
 \leq\sqrt{2n\alpha\gamma^2}.
\]
If \(n\leq1/(8\alpha\gamma^2)\), average testing error is at least
\(1/4\). Classifying by the output's sign, a classification error
incurs gap at least \(b\gamma\). Thus
\[
 \max_v\mathbb E_v[C_v(\hat x)-C_v^\ast]\geq b\gamma/4.
\]
In particular, for \(0<\epsilon\leq b/16\),
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 n&\leq \frac{b^2}{512\alpha\epsilon^2}\\
 &\Longrightarrow\
 \max_v\mathbb E_v[C_v(\hat x)-C_v^\ast]\geq2\epsilon.
 \end{aligned}
\]
This single-agent obstruction does not exclude distribution-specific
gains, threshold localization, or savings under richer advice
\ledger{7, 9}.

\subsection{P's sampling residual improves without predictions}

\begin{claim}
Comparing values of the expected empirical objective replaces P's
sampling residual \(O(e_s/\delta_{\min})\) by \(O(e_s)\).
The limiting residual becomes \(O(\delta_{\max}+e_s)\).
\end{claim}

For deterministic batch sizes, define
\[
 F_{i,k}(x)=\mathbb E\widehat C^i_{s_k^i}(x),
 \qquad
 b_{i,k}=\frac{U_i\sqrt{2\pi}}{\alpha_i\sqrt{s_k^i}}.
\]
Empirical CVaR is convex and \(L_i\)-Lipschitz. Expectation of a
minimum is no larger than the minimum of the expectation.
Together with P's DKW calculation, this gives
\[
 0\leq C^i(x)-F_{i,k}(x)\leq b_{i,k}.
\]
This is a deterministic expected-objective bound, uniform in its
constant, not a simultaneous bound on every realized empirical
function.

Let \(F_{i,k,\delta}\) denote ball smoothing on P's contracted
feasible set. P's estimator satisfies
\[
 \mathbb E[\hat g_k^i\mid\mathcal F_k]
 =\nabla F_{i,k,\delta}(y_k^i).
\]
Jensen's inequality, the one-sided bias, and Lipschitz continuity imply
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 F_{i,k,\delta}(x)&\geq C^i(x)-b_{i,k},\\
 F_{i,k,\delta}(z)&\leq C^i(z)+L_i\delta_i.
 \end{aligned}
\]
Consequently,
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 &\sum_i
 \langle\nabla F_{i,k,\delta}(y_k^i),y_k^i-z\rangle\\
 &\quad\geq
 m\bigl(\mathcal C(\bar x_k)-\mathcal C(z)\bigr)
 -L_{\max}\sum_j\|x_k^j-\bar x_k\|\\
 &\qquad-\sum_i(b_{i,k}+L_i\delta_i).
 \end{aligned}
\]
P's remaining disagreement and projection argument is unchanged.

Writing
\[
 \begin{gathered}
 S_T=\sum_{k<T}\eta_k,\qquad
 B_k=\frac1m\sum_i b_{i,k},\\
 \overline{L\delta}=\frac1m\sum_iL_i\delta_i,
 \qquad G_i=\frac{dU_i}{\delta_i},
 \end{gathered}
\]
the sharpened ergodic bound is
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E[\mathcal C(\hat x_T)-\mathcal C^\ast]
 \leq{}&
 \frac{D_x^2}{2S_T}
 +\frac{\sum_iG_i^2}{2mS_T}\sum_{k<T}\eta_k^2\\
 &+\frac{L_{\max}}{mS_T}
 \sum_{k<T}\eta_k
 \mathbb E\!\left[\sum_j\|x_k^j-\bar x_k\|\right]\\
 &+\overline{L\delta}
 +\frac1{S_T}\sum_{k<T}\eta_kB_k
 +\frac{D_xL_{\max}\delta_{\max}}r.
 \end{aligned}
\]
For fixed batch sizes, P's almost-sure limit minimizes the fixed
expected empirical smoothed objective. Its true gap is bounded
pathwise by
\[
 \overline{L\delta}+B+
 \frac{D_xL_{\max}\delta_{\max}}r,
 \qquad B=\frac1m\sum_i b_i.
\]
P's sampling condition makes \(B=O(e_s)\). Inverse smoothing radii
remain in the transient estimator term. Predictable batch sizes
require conditional expected objectives and expectations around random
bias bounds \ledger{2, 5}.

The derivation is in
\url{ada/quantile-obstruction-and-baseline.md},
lines 76--149. Levy et al.\ already analyze expected minibatch CVaR
objectives and give the sharper bias order
\[
 b_{i,k}
 =O\!\left(
 U_i\min\{1,(\alpha_i s_k^i)^{-1/2}\}
 \right).
\]
A shift handles signed bounded losses. The retained contribution is
the transfer to P, not a new expected-minibatch principle
\ledger{4, 5, 11, 52}.

\subsection{Finite-batch prediction correction can destroy convexity}

QuEst uses a correction of the form
\[
 \widehat Q_\lambda
 =Q(F_n)+\lambda
 \bigl[Q(\widetilde F_N^u)-Q(\widetilde F_n)\bigr].
\]
Its asymptotic inference theorem does not automatically provide a
convex objective for P's finite-batch optimizer.

Take \(x\in[-1,1]\), \(\alpha=1/2\), one labeled sample, and two
independent cheap samples. Let the true loss be deterministic,
\(J(x)=\mu x^2\), while a predicted loss equals \(x^2\) or \(1\),
equiprobably. Then
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E\widehat C^{\mathrm{pred}}_1(x)
 &=\frac{x^2+1}{2},\\
 \mathbb E\widehat C^{\mathrm{pred}}_2(x)
 &=\frac{x^2}{4}+\frac34.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The expected corrected objective is
\[
 \mu x^2+\frac{\lambda}{4}(1-x^2).
\]
For \(0<\mu<\lambda/4\), this is strictly concave and selects
endpoints, whereas the true objective selects zero. Smoothing retains
the quadratic curvature.

This is a transfer obstruction, not a contradiction of QuEst's
asymptotic theorem or an analysis of its population variance-optimal
weight. See
\url{ada/quantile-obstruction-and-baseline.md},
lines 165--194 \ledger{6, 8, 11, 52}.

\section{Retained continuation: costed sample-level screening}

\subsection{The stronger interface}

In the affine family \(J(x)=B(a+bSx)\), a scenario contains
\[
 (B,S,H)\in\{0,1\}\times\{-1,1\}\times\{0,1\},
 \qquad \Pr(B=1)=\alpha.
\]
A draw costs \(c_0>0\), optional binary prediction \(H\) costs
\(c_p\geq0\), and a true evaluation costs \(c_e>0\).
Opaque handles reveal nothing else about \(B,S\). Evaluating at
\(x=1\) reveals \(B\) and \(BS\); reevaluation supplies no fresh
sign. The prevalence \(p=\Pr(H=1)\) is known as part of this
interface. Learning it is not included in the fixed-rule theorem.

The sign may depend arbitrarily on \(B,H\). Define
\[
 C(x)=a+b\mu x,\qquad
 \mu=\frac{\mathbb E[BS]}{\alpha}.
\]
If \(\hat x\) minimizes \(b\hat\mu x\) on \([-1,1]\), then
\[
 C(\hat x)-C^\ast\leq2b|\hat\mu-\mu|.
\]
See \url{ada/two-resource-screening.md},
lines 28--72 \ledger{13--15}.

\subsection{A defensive finite-sample rule}

Query every predicted positive and each predicted negative with
independent probability \(\tau\in(0,1]\). Put
\[
 f=\Pr(B=1,H=0),\qquad \pi_1=1,\qquad\pi_0=\tau,
\]
and use
\[
 \hat\mu_N=\frac1{N\alpha}
 \sum_{j=1}^N\frac{I_jB_jS_j}{\pi_{H_j}}.
\]
Inverse-propensity cancellation gives
\[
 \mathbb E\hat\mu_N=\mu,\qquad
 \operatorname{Var}(\hat\mu_N)
 \leq\frac{\alpha-f+f/\tau}{N\alpha^2}.
\]
Expected evaluations and cost are
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 M_{\mathrm{true}}&=N[p+\tau(1-p)],\\
 K&=Nw,\qquad
 w=c_0+c_p+c_e[p+\tau(1-p)].
 \end{aligned}
\]
Hence
\[
 \mathbb E[C(\hat x)-C^\ast]
 \leq\frac{2b}{\alpha}
 \sqrt{\frac{\alpha-f+f/\tau}{N}}.
\]
False negatives enter through \(f/\tau\), while false positives
increase cost through \(p\).

Define
\[
 \mathcal V(\tau)
 =\frac{w(\alpha-f+f/\tau)}{\alpha^2}.
\]
For \(N=\lfloor K/w\rfloor\) and \(K>w\),
\[
 \mathbb E[C(\hat x)-C^\ast]
 \leq2b\sqrt{\frac{\mathcal V(\tau)}{K-w}}.
\]
The algorithm need not know \(f\). A horizon involving \(f\) is a
certificate, not an implementable accuracy stopping rule.
See \url{ada/two-resource-screening.md},
lines 74--134 \ledger{13, 15, 20}.

For a fair prediction-free comparison, write
\[
 D_c=c_0+c_e,\qquad A=c_0+c_p+c_ep.
\]
Fix \(\lambda\in(0,1]\). Skip prediction if \(A>D_c\); otherwise
set \(\tau=\lambda A/D_c\). Since
\[
 \frac w\tau=\frac{D_c}{\lambda}+c_e(1-p),
\]
and \(f\leq\alpha\),
\[
 \mathcal V(\tau)
 \leq(1+1/\lambda)\frac{D_c}{\alpha}.
\]
For exact labels \(H=B\), \(p=\alpha\), \(f=0\), and
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathcal V(\tau)
 &=\frac A\alpha
 \left(1+\lambda\frac{c_e(1-\alpha)}{D_c}\right)\\
 &\leq(1+\lambda)\frac A\alpha.
 \end{aligned}
\]
Including the branch that skips prediction,
\[
 \mathcal V_{\mathrm{rule}}
 \leq(1+\lambda)\frac{\min\{D_c,A\}}{\alpha}
 \qquad(H=B).
\]
These are sufficient cost--variance certificates. Their factors enter
fixed-budget error through a square root; they are not CVaR competitive
ratios or an optimal trust frontier. See
\url{ada/two-resource-screening.md},
lines 136--209 \ledger{14, 15, 20}.

\subsection{Matched affine resource endpoints}

With exact labels \(H=B\), worst-case small-error orders within this
opaque-scenario interface are
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E N_{\mathrm{draw}}
 &=\Theta\!\left(\frac{b^2}{\alpha\epsilon^2}\right),\\
 \mathbb E N_{\mathrm{true}}
 &=\Theta\!\left(\frac{b^2}{\epsilon^2}\right).
 \end{aligned}
\]
With optional paid prediction, optimal total-cost order is
\[
 \Theta\!\left[
 \frac{b^2}{\epsilon^2}
 \min\left\{
 \frac{c_0+c_e}{\alpha},
 \frac{c_0+c_p}{\alpha}+c_e
 \right\}\right].
\]

For the lower bound, take \(S\) independent of \(B\) with
\(\mathbb E_vS=v\gamma\). If \(R\) counts distinct evaluated tail
scenarios, transcript divergence is at most
\(4\gamma^2\mathbb E_+R\). Testing requires
\(\mathbb E_+R=\Omega(b^2/\epsilon^2)\), whereas
\[
 \mathbb ER\leq\alpha\mathbb EN_{\mathrm{draw}},
 \qquad
 \mathbb ER\leq\mathbb EN_{\mathrm{true}}.
\]
Evaluating a fixed number of observed tails attains both resource
orders. Integrable stopping times are handled by truncation.

Partition informative observations into \(R_1\), preceded by prediction,
and \(R_0\), obtained without prediction. Expected cost is at least
\[
 \frac{c_0+c_p+\alpha c_e}{\alpha}\mathbb ER_1
 +\frac{c_0+c_e}{\alpha}\mathbb ER_0.
\]
Opaque handles prevent selection of promising unpaid scenarios.
Combining this with the information bound gives the stated minimum.
Constant uninformative advice instead gives
\[
 \Theta\!\left(
 \frac{b^2(c_0+c_e)}{\alpha\epsilon^2}\right).
\]
See \url{ada/two-resource-screening.md},
lines 211--286 \ledger{15, 20}.

No matching intermediate-\(f\) lower bound was established. Advice
can reveal sign information independently of its tail-label error.
Exact labels save \(1/\alpha\) in costly evaluations, not draws;
the largest monetary saving requires cheap draws and predictions
\ledger{19}.

\section{Retained continuation: correction before minimization}

\subsection{The fixed convex lifted target}

After shifting losses to \(0\leq J\leq U\), define
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \phi(x,t;\xi)&=t+\frac{(J(x,\xi)-t)_+}{\alpha},\\
 \Phi(x,t)&=\mathbb E\phi(x,t;\xi),
 \qquad t\in[0,U].
 \end{aligned}
\]
This function is jointly convex, and
\[
 \min_{x,t}\Phi(x,t)=C^\ast,\qquad
 C(x)-C^\ast\leq\Phi(x,t)-\Phi^\ast.
\]
One true loss evaluates \(\phi\), without an empirical-CVaR batch.

Write \(z=(x,t)\), \(p=d+1\), and center \(t\) at \(U/2\) when
contracting the domain. Suitable bounds are
\[
 \begin{gathered}
 R_z=\sqrt{D_x^2+U^2},\qquad
 r_z=\min\{r,U/2\},\\
 M_\phi=U/\alpha,\qquad
 K_\phi=\sqrt{L^2+1}/\alpha.
 \end{gathered}
\]
Here \(R_z\) is a diameter bound, \(r_z\) an interior radius,
\(0\leq\phi\leq M_\phi\), and \(K_\phi\) a Lipschitz bound.

A clipped paired prediction supplies \(\hat\phi\). Choose a causal
query probability \(\pi>0\) from cheap information, with an independent
query coin conditional on that information and the scenario. Return
\[
 Z_{\mathrm{corr}}
 =\hat\phi+\frac I\pi(\phi-\hat\phi).
\]
Then
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E_I Z_{\mathrm{corr}}&=\phi,\\
 \mathbb E_I Z_{\mathrm{corr}}^2
 &=\phi^2+(\pi^{-1}-1)(\phi-\hat\phi)^2.
 \end{aligned}
\]
Consequently,
\[
 g_k=\frac p\delta
 Z_{\mathrm{corr}}(z_k+\delta u_k)u_k
\]
has conditional mean \(\nabla\Phi_\delta(z_k)\).
Predictably changing predictions do not change this fixed convex
target. Correction precedes threshold minimization; the realized
corrected sample need not itself be convex.
See \url{ada/lifted-correction-and-baselines.md},
lines 16--154 \ledger{16, 20}.

\subsection{Finite-time and cost guarantees}

If \(\pi\geq\nu>0\) and, including query-point randomness,
\[
 \mathbb E[(\phi-\hat\phi)^2\mid\mathcal F_k]\leq\sigma_k^2,
\]
then
\[
 \mathbb E[\|g_k\|^2\mid\mathcal F_k]
 \leq\frac{p^2}{\delta^2}
 \left[M_\phi^2+(\nu^{-1}-1)\sigma_k^2\right].
\]
Squared loss-prediction error divided by \(\alpha^2\) bounds the
residual, and clipping gives \(\sigma_k^2\leq M_\phi^2\).

Constant-step projected updates satisfy
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E[C(\bar x_T)-C^\ast]
 \leq{}&
 \frac{R_z^2}{2\eta T}\\
 &+\frac{\eta p^2}{2\delta^2}
 \left[M_\phi^2+(\nu^{-1}-1)\bar\sigma_T^2\right]\\
 &+K_\phi\delta(1+R_z/r_z),
 \end{aligned}
\]
where \(\bar\sigma_T^2=T^{-1}\sum_{k<T}\mathbb E\sigma_k^2\).

Unknown residual error need not determine the step. With
\[
 S_k=\sum_{j=0}^k\|g_j\|^2,\qquad
 \eta_k=R_z/\sqrt{S_k},
\]
and zero updates when \(S_k=0\), the pathwise regret inequality is
\[
 \sum_{k<T}\langle g_k,z_k-z\rangle
 \leq\frac32R_z\sqrt{S_{T-1}}.
\]
Since \(z_k\) precedes the current sample, conditional unbiasedness gives
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E[C(\bar x_T)-C^\ast]
 \leq{}&
 \frac{3R_zp}{2\delta\sqrt T}
 \sqrt{M_\phi^2+(\nu^{-1}-1)\bar\sigma_T^2}\\
 &+K_\phi\delta(1+R_z/r_z).
 \end{aligned}
\]
This is a finite-time policy. A separate square-summable
diminishing-step policy, with fixed smoothing and a positive propensity
floor, supports convergence to the smoothed lifted minimizer
\ledger{16}.

For charged cost let
\[
 A_{\mathrm{lift}}=c_0+c_p,\qquad D_c=c_0+c_e.
\]
Skip prediction if \(A_{\mathrm{lift}}>D_c\); otherwise set
\[
 \nu=\lambda A_{\mathrm{lift}}/D_c,\qquad
 w=A_{\mathrm{lift}}+\nu c_e.
\]
The certificate
\[
 \mathcal W_{\mathrm{lift}}
 =w\left[M_\phi^2+(\nu^{-1}-1)\sigma^2\right]
\]
obeys
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathcal W_{\mathrm{lift}}
 &\leq(1+\lambda)A_{\mathrm{lift}}M_\phi^2
 &&\text{for exact predictions},\\
 \mathcal W_{\mathrm{lift}}
 &\leq(1+1/\lambda)D_cM_\phi^2
 &&\text{for arbitrary predictions}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
These compare sufficient certificates at common geometry and smoothing,
not optimal instancewise costs.
See \url{ada/lifted-correction-and-baselines.md},
lines 156--226 \ledger{16, 19}.

\section{Retained gradient-estimator comparisons}

These computations remain valid sufficient upper bounds. Entry 44
corrects their interpretation: they compare gradient-estimator
templates, not the best prediction-free noisy-value methods
\ledger{18, 20, 42, 44}.

For one agent, the sharpened batched one-point analysis of P gives
\[
 O\!\left(
 \frac{dUD_x}{\delta\sqrt T}
 +L\delta(1+D_x/r)
 +\frac U{\sqrt{\alpha s}}\right).
\]
Balancing terms gives total evaluations
\[
 O\!\left(
 \frac{d^2U^4D_x^2L^2}{\alpha\epsilon^6}\right),
\]
suppressing fixed contraction factors.

A prediction-free one-sample lifted method instead gives
\[
 O\!\left(
 \frac{p^2R_z^2M_\phi^2K_\phi^2(1+R_z/r_z)^2}
 {\epsilon^4}\right).
\]
It removes empirical-CVaR bias, but its displayed dependence on
\(\alpha\) may be worse.

For independent batches on the two sides, let
\[
 g=\frac d{2\delta}
 \left[
 \widehat C_s(x+\delta u;S^+)
 -\widehat C_s(x-\delta u;S^-)
 \right]u.
\]
Its mean is \(\nabla F_{s,\delta}(x)\), where
\(F_s=\mathbb E\widehat C_s\), and
\[
 \mathbb E\|g\|^2
 \leq d^2L^2+\frac{d^2v_s}{2\delta^2},
 \qquad
 v_s=\sup_x\operatorname{Var}(\widehat C_s(x)).
\]
Levy et al.\ give \(v_s=O(U^2/(\alpha s))\). Taking
\(s\) of order \(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2)\) and
\(\delta\) of order \(\epsilon/L\) yields
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 T&=O(d^2D_x^2L^2/\epsilon^2),\\
 2sT&=O\!\left(
 \frac{d^2D_x^2L^2U^2}{\alpha\epsilon^4}\right).
 \end{aligned}
\]
No common-random-number oracle is assumed. The affine family is easier:
a value at \(x=1\) reveals its sampled slope, and uniform sampling
already uses \(O(b^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2))\) evaluations.

See \url{ada/lifted-correction-and-baselines.md},
lines 228--321. The noisy-value baseline below supersedes any global
optimality interpretation of these exponents.

\section{Retained third round: moving tails and consistent ranks}

\subsection{Why population labels are insufficient for empirical tails}

A batch may contain fewer than \(\lceil\alpha s\rceil\) strict
population-tail observations. Its empirical CVaR then also depends on
observations below the population quantile. Exact population labels
do not identify them; completing the batch on this event can cost a
constant fraction of all values \ledger{22, 23, 29}.

The third round instead declared a within-batch score order. Let
sample losses be convex, \(L\)-Lipschitz, and in \([0,U]\), and write
\[
 F_n(x)=\mathbb E\widehat C_n(x),\qquad
 0\leq C(x)-F_n(x)\leq
 c_bU\min\{1,(\alpha n)^{-1/2}\}.
\]
A score rule, fixed conditional on the past, orders fresh scenarios.
Full and half batches use restrictions of the same order, with
consistent independent tie-breaking. The order may depend on the
decision and be arbitrarily inaccurate. Independently supplied
inconsistent sub-batch lists are excluded.

Draws, prediction including sorting, and true queries are charged.
A zeroth-order query returns a loss. A first-order query also returns
a measurable subgradient of norm at most \(L\), which is strictly
stronger than P's interface. Same-decision values may be reused in
sub-batches; cross-decision reuse is not assumed.
See \url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md},
lines 43--90 \ledger{24, 26}.

\subsection{A multilevel variance bound without rank accuracy}

For an ordered batch of size \(m\), let \(q\) assign weight
\(1/(\alpha m)\) to a top prefix until total weight one is reached,
with a fractional boundary weight. For \(\|v_j\|\leq G\), define
\[
 A_m=\sum_jq_jv_j,\qquad
 \Delta_m=A_m-\frac12
 \left(A_{m/2}^{(1)}+A_{m/2}^{(2)}\right).
\]
For true ranks, \(v_j\) can be sample values or a fixed measurable
selection of sample subgradients. Predicted ranks give feasible
weights, though generally not maximizing ones.

Full weights and half weights divided by two have the same coordinate
cap. Their supports within each half are nested prefixes. If \(W\)
is the full-batch weight in the first half, then
\[
 \left\|q-\frac12(q^{(1)}+q^{(2)})\right\|_1
 =2|W-1/2|.
\]
Exchangeability of the weight multiset gives
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb EW&=\frac12,\\
 \operatorname{Var}(W)
 &=\frac{m}{4(m-1)}
 \sum_j(q_j-1/m)^2\\
 &\leq\frac1{4\alpha(m-1)}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
Thus, for \(m\geq2\),
\[
 \mathbb E\|\Delta_m\|^2
 \leq4G^2\operatorname{Var}(W)
 \leq\frac{2G^2}{\alpha m}.
\]
The vector bound is pathwise; scores need not be independent of
losses or gradients. Fractional weights are included. The result
extends the true-rank sign argument to arbitrary consistent orders,
not arbitrary masks.
See \url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md},
lines 92--146 \ledger{24, 26}.

\subsection{Multilevel responses and full-batch correction}

Set
\[
 n_0=\lceil1/\alpha\rceil,\qquad
 n=2^\ell n_0,\qquad \ell\geq1,
\]
and draw \(R\) with
\[
 p_j=\Pr(R=j)=
 \begin{cases}
 2^{-j},&1\leq j<\ell,\\
 2^{1-\ell},&j=\ell.
 \end{cases}
\]
Using \(m_R=2^Rn_0\) scenarios, return
\[
 Z=A_{n_0}+\frac{\Delta_{m_R}}{p_R}.
\]
The base uses the first \(n_0\) elements of the same batch.
True-rank telescoping yields
\[
 \mathbb EZ=F_n(x)
 \quad\text{or}\quad
 \mathbb EZ\in\partial F_n(x),
\]
for scalar or subgradient responses. For both true and predicted
consistent ranks,
\[
 \mathbb E\|Z\|^2\leq V_G,\qquad
 V_G=G^2\left(2+\frac{4\ell}{\alpha n_0}\right),
\]
and expected draws are \(M=n_0(\ell+1)\).

The union of predicted base, full, and half supports has size at most
\[
 \lceil\alpha n_0\rceil+\lceil\alpha m_R\rceil
 +2\lceil\alpha m_R/2\rceil
 \leq5\alpha m_R,
\]
and at most \(m_R\). The predicted response \(\widehat Z\) therefore
requires at most \(hM\) expected true queries, where
\(h=\min\{1,5\alpha\}\).

Audit the whole batch independently with probability \(\tau\), returning
\[
 Z^{\mathrm{corr}}
 =\widehat Z+\frac I\tau(Z-\widehat Z).
\]
Conditional on the batch,
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E_I Z^{\mathrm{corr}}&=Z,\\
 \mathbb E_I\|Z^{\mathrm{corr}}\|^2
 &=\|Z\|^2+(\tau^{-1}-1)\|Z-\widehat Z\|^2.
 \end{aligned}
\]
With
\[
 e_k^2=\mathbb E[\|Z-\widehat Z\|^2\mid\mathcal F_k],
\]
one obtains
\[
 \mathbb E[\|Z^{\mathrm{corr}}\|^2\mid\mathcal F_k]
 \leq V_G+(\tau^{-1}-1)e_k^2,\qquad
 e_k^2\leq4V_G.
\]
Exact agreement of the required weights gives \(e_k=0\).

For \(E_m=A_m-\widehat A_m\), the error retains cross-scale structure:
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 e_k^2\leq{}&
 2\mathbb E\|E_{n_0}\|^2\\
 &+2\sum_{j=1}^{\ell}\frac1{p_j}
 \mathbb E\left\|
 E_{m_j}
 -\frac{E_{m_j/2}^{(1)}+E_{m_j/2}^{(2)}}2
 \right\|^2.
 \end{aligned}
\]
Each discrepancy is at most \(G\|q-\widehat q\|_1\).
The corrected mean remains the fixed true \(F_n\), despite predictable
changes in the score rule.
See \url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md},
lines 148--243 \ledger{26}.

\subsection{Optimization and charged-cost upper bounds}

The observed-gradient-norm step rule gives
\[
 \mathbb E[C(\bar x_T)-C^\ast]
 \leq b_n+\frac{3D_x}{2\sqrt T}
 \sqrt{V_L+(\tau^{-1}-1)\bar e_T^2},
\]
where
\[
 b_n=c_bU\min\{1,(\alpha n)^{-1/2}\},\qquad
 \bar e_T^2=\frac1T\sum_{k<T}\mathbb Ee_k^2.
\]
With exact ranks, \(n\) of order \(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2)\), and
\(\tau=O(\alpha)\),
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 T&=\widetilde O(D_x^2L^2/\epsilon^2),\\
 N_{\mathrm{draw}}
 &=\widetilde O(D_x^2L^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2)),\\
 N_{\mathrm{true}}
 &=\widetilde O(D_x^2L^2/\epsilon^2).
 \end{aligned}
\]
The comparator is Levy's prediction-free multilevel first-order method,
which pays true-query cost on all draws. This is an upper-bound
comparison under richer charged advice \ledger{27}.

For independent scalar responses at \(x+\delta u\) and
\(x-\delta u\), set
\[
 g=\frac d{2\delta}
 (Z^{\mathrm{corr}}_+-Z^{\mathrm{corr}}_-)u.
\]
Its mean is \(\nabla F_{n,\delta}(x)\), and
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E[C(\bar x_T)-C^\ast]
 \leq{}&b_n+L\delta(1+D_x/r)\\
 &+\frac{3D_xd}{2\delta\sqrt T}
 \sqrt{V_U+(\tau^{-1}-1)\bar e_T^2}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
Under exact ranks and \(\tau=O(\alpha)\), sufficient counts are
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 N_{\mathrm{draw}}
 &=\widetilde O\!\left(
 \frac{d^2D_x^2U^2L^2(1+D_x/r)^2}
 {\alpha\epsilon^4}\right),\\
 N_{\mathrm{true}}
 &=\widetilde O\!\left(
 \frac{d^2D_x^2U^2L^2(1+D_x/r)^2}
 {\epsilon^4}\right).
 \end{aligned}
\]
The batch cap stays fixed throughout the run. These are
gradient-template bounds, not the noisy-value frontier.
See \url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md},
lines 245--333 \ledger{27, 42, 44}.

For charged resources, put
\[
 D_c=c_0+c_e,\qquad A_{\mathrm{rank}}=c_0+c_p+h c_e.
\]
Skip advice if \(A_{\mathrm{rank}}>D_c\); otherwise use
\(\tau=\lambda A_{\mathrm{rank}}/D_c\). Expected response cost is
at most \(w=M(A_{\mathrm{rank}}+\tau c_e)\), and
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 wV_G&\leq(1+\lambda)MA_{\mathrm{rank}}V_G
 &&\text{for exact ranks},\\
 w\left[V_G+(\tau^{-1}-1)\bar e_T^2\right]
 &\leq4(1+1/\lambda)MD_cV_G
 &&\text{for arbitrary ranks}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The strongest costly-query saving at fixed \(\lambda\) requires
\(c_0+c_p=O(\alpha c_e)\). These are expected-cost,
fixed-horizon guarantees. Deterministic-budget stopping and an
error-dependent accuracy certificate were not proved for this
construction.
See \url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md},
lines 335--386 \ledger{28}.

\section{Retained interface distinctions and objections}

\subsection{Exact population-tail witnesses give a separate endpoint}

Suppose a cheap Bernoulli label has conditional probability
\(a_x(\xi)\in[0,1]\) satisfying
\[
 \mathbb Ea_x=\alpha,\qquad
 \mathbb E[a_xJ(x,\xi)]=\alpha C(x).
\]
This is an exact CVaR dual witness, with appropriate randomization at
quantile atoms. Draw until a positive label and then query a sample
subgradient. Its conditional mean is a subgradient of \(C\), because
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 C(y)
 &\geq\alpha^{-1}\mathbb E[a_xJ(y,\xi)]\\
 &\geq C(x)+
 \left\langle
 \alpha^{-1}\mathbb E[a_xg(x,\xi)],y-x
 \right\rangle.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The accepted gradient has norm at most \(L\). Stochastic subgradient
descent therefore uses
\[
 O(D_x^2L^2/\epsilon^2)
 \quad\text{costly gradients},\qquad
 O(D_x^2L^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2))
 \quad\text{draws}.
\]
The affine family has \(a_x=B\) everywhere; its lower bound survives
even if a query reveals the whole sampled affine function.
This matches the resource orders in the population-witness first-order
interface. It neither learns the witness nor tolerates wrong witnesses.
See \url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md},
lines 388--421 \ledger{29}.

\subsection{Full ranks invalidate the old costly-query lower bound}

At \(x=1\), the affine losses take only values
\(\{0,a-b,a+b\}\). After receiving their exact order, binary search
for category boundaries recovers all counts with \(O(\log n)\)
true evaluations. Thus the old \(\Omega(b^2/\epsilon^2)\)
costly-query bound does not transfer from population labels to full
rankings.

A draw lower bound survives when advice contains only the actual order
of independent sampled losses. Extra numerical population information
would change the model. The fifth round below supplies a new costly-query
hard family instead of reusing the invalidated argument.
See \url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md},
lines 423--439 \ledger{25, 32, 47}.

\subsection{Fixed masks preserve convexity; moving masks need not}

For nonnegative losses and a scenario mask \(M(\xi)\in\{0,1\}\)
fixed across decisions, \(M(\xi)J(x,\xi)\) remains convex. If
\(C_M\) is its CVaR and \(a_x\) an optimal true-tail witness, then
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 0\leq C(x)-C_M(x)
 &\leq\alpha^{-1}\mathbb E[a_x(1-M)J(x,\xi)]\\
 &\leq Uf(x)/\alpha,\qquad
 f(x)=\mathbb E[a_x(1-M)].
 \end{aligned}
\]
A uniform bound \(f(x)\leq f\) adds at most \(Uf/\alpha\) when
optimizing the screened objective. The same dual argument applies to
fixed-mask empirical objectives.

For equiprobable losses \(1+x\) and \(1-x\), with \(\alpha=1/2\),
keeping only the smaller loss instead produces \(1-|x|\), whereas
true CVaR is \(1+|x|\). Arbitrary moving masks therefore need not
preserve convexity.
See \url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md},
lines 441--475 \ledger{29}.

\subsection{Quantiles can improve localization}

Ignoring equality atoms, a lifted sample subgradient is
\[
 v=\left(\frac{ag}{\alpha},\,1-\frac a\alpha\right),
 \qquad a=\mathbf1\{J(x,\xi)>t\}.
\]
For \(p_t=\Pr(J(x,\xi)>t)\),
\[
 \mathbb E\|v\|^2
 \leq1-\frac{2p_t}{\alpha}
 +\frac{(L^2+1)p_t}{\alpha^2}.
\]
Maintaining \(p_t=O(\alpha)\) improves the crude moment dependence
to \(O((L^2+1)/\alpha)\). Exact exceedance labels also permit skipping
gradients when \(a=0\), but maintaining localization as decisions move
is an additional obligation.

For an exact quantile \(q(x)\),
\[
 q(x)+\frac{(J(x,\xi)-q(x))_+}{\alpha}
\]
has mean \(C(x)\), variance at most \(U^2/\alpha\), and requires a
true evaluation only on strict exceedances if those labels are supplied.
An arbitrary threshold changes the mean and can lose convexity in
\(x\). Under a density bound \(f_x\leq K\) between \(q(x)\) and \(t\),
threshold excess is at most
\[
 \frac{K(t-q(x))^2}{2\alpha};
\]
without that regularity, only a first-order bound follows.
See \url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md},
lines 477--514 \ledger{29}.

\subsection{A fixed trust curve is not an impossibility frontier}

For stationary affine binary advice, uniformly evaluate \(K^{1/2}\)
pilot scenarios at nominal budget \(K\). Estimate frequencies and
tail second moments, then optimize the estimated certificate over
propensities in \([K^{-1/8},1]^2\), including the prediction-free
alternative.

The certificate has moment-Lipschitz constant \(O(K^{1/8})\);
pilot estimation error is \(O(K^{-1/4})\). Uniform certificate
error is therefore \(O(K^{-1/8})=o(1)\). The propensity floor
vanishes and pilot cost is \(o(K)\), for fixed rarity and costs.
Fresh data preserve conditional unbiasedness. Conservative cost
estimation, or expected total cost \(K(1+o(1))\), lets the rule
approach the best fixed allocation.

This is an asymptotic adaptive-stratification argument, not a
finite-budget frontier uniform in varying rarity and costs.
See \url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md},
lines 516--543 \ledger{29}.

\section{Retained fourth round: capped rank quadrature}

\subsection{Fractional CVaR weights and monotone integration}

At a chosen decision, draw independent scenario handles, receive a
permutation, and pay for selected values. The permutation is frozen
before fresh integration probes. Values are cached at that decision;
distinct value requests use independent batches. Prediction and sorting
are charged. This is stronger than P's sampling interface
\ledger{36, 38}.

For correctly sorted losses \(a_1\leq\cdots\leq a_s\) in \([0,U]\),
put \(q_s(v)=a_{\lceil sv\rceil}\). Empirical CVaR is exactly
\[
 A_s=\frac1\alpha\int_{1-\alpha}^1q_s(v)\,dv
 =\int_0^1q_s(1-\alpha+\alpha u)\,du.
\]
Fractional boundary weights are included.

One independent uniform probe in each of \(k\) equal cells gives a
conditionally unbiased estimator. If \(r_j\) is the cell range,
monotonicity gives \(\sum_jr_j\leq U\), and
\[
 \operatorname{Var}(\widehat A_s\mid a_{1:s})
 \leq\frac1{4k^2}\sum_jr_j^2
 \leq\frac{U^2}{4k^2}.
\]
It uses at most \(k\) costly values. Deterministic equally spaced
quadrature instead has error \(O(U/k)\) and needs a bias analysis.

Together with empirical bias \(O(U/\sqrt{\alpha s})\) and variance
\(O(U^2/(\alpha s))\), the choices
\[
 s\asymp U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2),\qquad k\asymp U/\epsilon
\]
give costly-query order \(\epsilon^{-3}\) within independent two-point
optimization, with draw order \(1/(\alpha\epsilon^4)\) at fixed
remaining parameters. Wrong predicted tails need not preserve the mean.
See \url{ada/rank-quadrature-and-robust-lifting.md},
lines 71--115 \ledger{34, 37}.

\subsection{Capped adaptive integration}

For a step function \(f:[0,1]\to[0,M]\), start with \(k\) equal
cells and query endpoints. Bisect \([a,b]\) whenever
\[
 (b-a)|f(b)-f(a)|>M/k^2,
\]
until no cell qualifies or \(2k\) bisections have occurred.
Independently sample a fresh uniform point \(V_I\) in each final
cell \(I\), returning
\[
 Q=\sum_I|I|f(V_I).
\]
There are at most \(3k\) final cells and \(6k+1\leq7k\) queries.
Repeated finite-array positions are cached.

For every ordering, fresh probes conditional on the pilot partition
give the exact integral mean. Every final width is at most \(1/k\), so
\[
 \mathbb E[Q\mid f]=\int f,\qquad
 \operatorname{Var}(Q\mid f)\leq\frac{M^2}{4k}.
\]
If \(f\) is nondecreasing, fewer than \(k/2^h\) cells can split at
relative depth \(h\): each has variation exceeding \(M2^h/k\), and
disjoint variations sum to at most \(M\). Fewer than \(2k\) splits
therefore suffice, regardless of the qualifying-cell order.

For each final cell put
\[
 A_I=|I|\bigl(f(\sup I)-f(\inf I)\bigr).
\]
Then
\[
 \max_I A_I\leq M/k^2,\qquad
 \sum_I A_I\leq M/k,
\]
and
\[
 \operatorname{Var}(Q\mid f)
 \leq\frac14\sum_I A_I^2
 \leq\frac{M^2}{4k^3}.
\]
Consistent endpoint conventions handle jumps. Applied to an exact
empirical tail quantile, this gives
\(k\asymp(U/\epsilon)^{2/3}\), and costly-query order
\(\epsilon^{-8/3}\) within the two-point template.

Adaptive monotone integration with inverse-cube variance is classical.
See \url{ada/rank-quadrature-and-robust-lifting.md},
lines 117--183 \ledger{37--39}.

\subsection{Full lifted integration is unbiased under every permutation}

Center \(t\) at \(U/2\), write \(z=(x,w)\), \(t=w+U/2\), and use
\[
 \mathcal Z=\mathcal X\times[-U/2,U/2].
\]
For this construction define
\[
 \begin{gathered}
 p=d+1,\quad D=\sqrt{D_x^2+U^2},\quad R=\min\{r,U/2\},\\
 M=U/\alpha,\quad K=\sqrt{L^2+1}/\alpha,\quad
 H=K(1+D/R).
 \end{gathered}
\]
Here \(D\) is a diameter, not a cost.

For a predicted permutation \(\pi\), apply capped quadrature to
\[
 f_z(v)=\phi(z;\xi_{\pi(\lceil sv\rceil)}).
\]
For every batch and permutation,
\[
 \mathbb E[Q_s(z)\mid\xi_{1:s},\pi]
 =\frac1s\sum_{j=1}^s\phi(z;\xi_j).
\]
Thus \(\mathbb E Q_s(z)=F(z)\), where \(F=\mathbb E\phi\) is the
fixed true convex lifted objective. There is no empirical threshold
minimization, full-batch audit, or cross-sub-batch consistency
requirement.

Define
\[
 \chi(z)=\frac{4k}{M^2}
 \mathbb E\!\left[
 \operatorname{Var}(Q_s(z)\mid\xi_{1:s},\pi)
 \,\middle|\,\text{history}\right].
\]
Always \(0\leq\chi(z)\leq1\), while exact ranks give
\(\chi(z)\leq k^{-2}\). Total variance gives
\[
 \operatorname{Var}(Q_s(z)\mid\text{history})
 \leq\frac{M^2}{4s}+\frac{M^2\chi(z)}{4k}.
\]
See \url{ada/rank-quadrature-and-robust-lifting.md},
lines 185--249 \ledger{36, 38}.

\subsection{Error-sensitive optimization}

Project onto
\[
 \mathcal Z_\delta=(1-\delta/R)\mathcal Z,\qquad 0<\delta<R.
\]
Using independent ranked batches at the two perturbed points, set
\[
 g_j=\frac p{2\delta}
 [Q_s(z_j+\delta u_j)-Q_s(z_j-\delta u_j)]u_j.
\]
Then
\[
 \mathbb E[g_j\mid\mathcal F_j]=\nabla F_\delta(z_j),
\]
and, averaging the two roughness terms,
\[
 \mathbb E[\|g_j\|^2\mid\mathcal F_j]
 \leq p^2K^2+
 \frac{p^2M^2}{8\delta^2}
 \left(\frac1s+\frac{\bar\chi_j}{k}\right).
\]
The observed-gradient-norm rule yields
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E[C(\bar x_T)-C^\ast]
 \leq{}&H\delta\\
 &+\frac{3D}{2\sqrt T}
 \sqrt{
 p^2K^2+\frac{p^2M^2}{8\delta^2}
 \left(\frac1s+\frac{\overline\chi_T}{k}\right)
 },
 \end{aligned}
 \tag{1}
\]
where
\[
 \overline\chi_T=\frac1T\sum_{j=1}^T\mathbb E\bar\chi_j.
\]
Convexity gives \(F\leq F_\delta\); contraction and smoothing cost
at most \(H\delta\); and \(C(\bar x_T)\leq F(\bar z_T)\).

Under exact ranks choose
\[
 \begin{gathered}
 \delta\asymp\epsilon/H,\qquad
 k\asymp\left(\frac M{\delta K}\right)^{2/3},
 \qquad s=k^3,\\
 T\asymp p^2D^2K^2/\epsilon^2.
 \end{gathered}
\]
Then
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 N_{\mathrm{eval}}
 &=O\!\left(
 p^2D^2K^{4/3}M^{2/3}H^{2/3}
 \epsilon^{-8/3}\right),\\
 N_{\mathrm{draw}}
 &=O\!\left(
 p^2D^2M^2H^2\epsilon^{-4}\right).
 \end{aligned}
\]
These are valid upper bounds within the compared gradient templates.
They do not establish a general advantage over prediction-free
noisy-value optimization; their \(\alpha\)-dependence is also worse
than direct-tail quadrature.
See \url{ada/rank-quadrature-and-robust-lifting.md},
lines 251--328 \ledger{38--40, 42, 44}.

\subsection{Charged certificate and deterministic budget conversion}

Let
\[
 c=c_0+c_p,\qquad d_0=c_0+c_e,\qquad a=M/(\delta K).
\]
The comparator is the lifted independent two-point method with one
true sample per side:
\[
 \text{cost}=2d_0,\qquad
 V_0=p^2\left(K^2+\frac{M^2}{8\delta^2}\right).
\]
Skip ranks if \(a<1\) or \(c>d_0\). Otherwise set
\[
 k=\max\left\{
 1,\left\lfloor
 \min\{a^{2/3},\sqrt{d_0/c}\}
 \right\rfloor\right\},\qquad s=k^3.
\]
Declared response cost is bounded by
\[
 C_k=2(k^3c+7kc_e)\leq16kd_0.
\]
Writing \(\overline V_k\) for the moment bound in (1) and
\(\Gamma_0=2d_0V_0\),
\[
 \frac{C_k\overline V_k}{\Gamma_0}
 \leq8\left(\frac9{k^2}+\overline\chi_T\right).
 \tag{2}
\]
Indeed, the ratio is bounded by
\[
 8\,
 \frac{
 kK^2+\dfrac{M^2}{8\delta^2}
 (k^{-2}+\overline\chi_T)
 }{
 K^2+\dfrac{M^2}{8\delta^2}
 },
\]
and \(kK^2\leq M^2/(\delta^2k^2)\).
Arbitrary ranks therefore give a factor at most \(80\), and exact
ranks a factor at most \(80/k^2\). These are certificates against
the specified lifted gradient baseline, not competitive ratios.

For announced budget \(\mathfrak B\geq2C_k\), take
\(T=\lfloor\mathfrak B/C_k\rfloor\). Then
\[
 \mathbb E[C(\bar x_T)-C^\ast]
 \leq H\delta+
 \frac{3D}{\sqrt2}
 \sqrt{\frac{C_k\overline V_k}{\mathfrak B}}.
\]
The query cap gives deterministic declared response cost.

The uncapped rate requires
\[
 c\lesssim d_0(\delta K/M)^{4/3}.
\]
For fixed positive draw and prediction costs, \(k\) eventually reaches
its cost cap. The \(\epsilon^{-8/3}\) count is not a uniform
total-cost exponent. Returning to this gradient baseline's rate
does not mean returning to the best noisy-value rate.
See \url{ada/rank-quadrature-and-robust-lifting.md},
lines 330--403 \ledger{38--40, 42, 44}.

\subsection{Paid selection without known rank quality}

Use a finite geometric grid of \(N\) radii. Allocate training budget
\(\mathfrak B/(2N)\) to each admissible candidate and validate each
lifted output \(\widehat z_j\) using
\[
 n_v=\left\lfloor\frac{\mathfrak B}{2Nd_0}\right\rfloor
\]
fresh prediction-free samples of \(\phi\).
Selecting the smallest validation mean gives
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E[F(\widehat z_{\mathrm{selected}})-F^\ast]
 \leq{}&
 \min_j\mathbb E[F(\widehat z_j)-F^\ast]\\
 &+O\!\left(
 M\sqrt{\frac{\log(2N)}{n_v}}\right).
 \end{aligned}
\]
Conditional independence of training and validation supplies the bound.
A logarithmic grid retains the arbitrary-advice
\(\mathfrak B^{-1/4}\) and uncapped exact-advice
\(\mathfrak B^{-3/8}\) orders within these templates.

The direct-CVaR gradient baseline can be included as a paid candidate.
For independent empirical-CVaR validation,
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 &\mathbb E\max_j
 |\widehat C_{n_v}(\widehat x_j)-C(\widehat x_j)|\\
 &\quad\leq b_{n_v}
 +O\!\left(
 \frac U\alpha
 \sqrt{\frac{\log(2N)}{n_v}}\right),\\
 &b_{n_v}=O(U/\sqrt{\alpha n_v}).
 \end{aligned}
\]
Selection adds at most twice this error to the best candidate gap
at the allocated budget fraction. It does not change the comparator
in (2). This is fixed-budget selection, not a sequential certificate
for a prescribed true gap.
See \url{ada/rank-quadrature-and-robust-lifting.md},
lines 405--487 \ledger{38--40}.

\section{Retained fourth-round objections}

\begin{objection}
Multiplying a monotone-estimation lower bound by an optimization horizon
proves the \(\epsilon^{-8/3}\) exponent optimal.
\end{objection}

It does not. The estimation construction divides \([0,1]\) into \(m\)
blocks and independently places a jump of height \(U/m\) at either
the first or third quarter of each block. A query exposes at most its
block's bit. After \(q\) queries, posterior integral variance is at least
\[
 (m-q)\frac{U^2}{16m^4}.
\]
Taking \(m=2q\) gives \(\Omega(U^2/q^3)\). A finite array can
represent this inside an empirical CVaR tail. An optimizer can use
cross-decision structure, however, so the bound does not compose
with a gradient horizon. The fifth-round lower bound instead concerns
optimization directly \ledger{39, 47}.

\begin{objection}
A supports-only oracle automatically restores the affine costly-query
lower bound.
\end{objection}

A single support is weaker than a full order, but support queries on
chosen subsets or sufficiently rich nested supports can reveal
comparisons. The transcript must be specified before transferring a
lower bound \ledger{39}.

\begin{objection}
Loss-rank quadrature also improves first-order gradients.
\end{objection}

Subgradients need not be monotone in loss rank. Rank probes at selected
decisions can also reveal directions in affine examples. No general
analogous first-order quadrature improvement was established
\ledger{39}.

\begin{objection}
Wrong rankings must be detected or corrected by a full-batch audit.
\end{objection}

Full lifted integration has the correct mean under every permutation.
Width and query caps control cost and variance without certifying
sortedness. A tester alone does not repair direct-tail plug-in bias,
and no robust direct-tail sortedness-test theorem was proved.
See \url{ada/rank-quadrature-and-robust-lifting.md},
lines 489--561 \ledger{36, 38, 40}.

\section{Retained fifth round: the corrected noisy-value baseline}

Review 42 identified a substantive omission. Agarwal et al.\ study
stochastic convex optimization with independent centered sub-Gaussian
value noise. Belloni et al.\ give an approximate-value construction
using \(\widetilde O(p^{4.5})\) oracle calls and a noisy-value
application with normalized complexity
\[
 \widetilde O(p^{7.5}\epsilon^{-2}).
\]
Their construction uses discretization and a fixed approximate oracle.
Its uniform approximation requirement must not silently become a
pointwise adaptive-noise argument \ledger{42, 44}.

For bounded losses, one true evaluation supplies an unbiased observation
of
\[
 \phi(x,t;\xi)=t+(J(x,\xi)-t)_+/\alpha.
\]
The centered noise is uniformly sub-Gaussian because
\(0\leq\phi\leq U/\alpha\). Applying the noisy-value construction
in dimension \(p=d+1\), with range and geometry factors included,
gives prediction-free accuracy exponent \(2\) at fixed dimension.
This is a centralized oracle-complexity comparison, not a replacement
for P's distributed last-iterate theorem.

Thus the old \(\epsilon^{-8/3}\) and \(\epsilon^{-4}\) comparisons
do not identify a prediction-free frontier.
See \url{ada/exact-versus-untrusted-ranks.md},
lines 53--105, and the corrected baseline notes
\ledger{44, 49, 50}.

\section{The fifth-round information model}

Let the feasible set initially be an interval, later a box of fixed
dimension. Sample losses are convex, uniformly \(L\)-Lipschitz,
and in \([0,U]\), with a fixed independent scenario law.

At a chosen decision, draw a batch of handles, receive a permutation,
and pay for selected true values. All simulation randomness belongs to
the handle, so same-decision values can be cached. Exact ranks order
losses nondecreasingly, with a fixed tie rule encoding no additional
data. Arbitrary advice may return any permutation. No numerical
surrogate values, tie-equivalence classes, or population probabilities
are supplied.

Different value requests use fresh batches. Predictions may depend on
history but are fixed before current residual probes. Free ranks
compare losses at a common decision only. Free cross-decision
comparisons are excluded.

Draws, costly values, and prediction plus sorting are counted separately.
Lower bounds sometimes grant coordinates or arbitrary coordinate
probes. Uniform surrogate error is an analytical condition, not
information the algorithm needs.
See \url{ada/exact-versus-untrusted-ranks.md},
lines 107--139 \ledger{47, 48, 50}.

\section{Approximate convex search without a gradient horizon}

For convex \(L\)-Lipschitz \(f\) on an interval of length \(D\),
evaluate trisection points \(u<v\) with absolute errors at most \(\nu\).
Keep the left two thirds if the estimate at \(u\) is no larger;
otherwise keep the right two thirds. Retain all evaluated candidates
and their estimates, including a final midpoint, and output the
candidate with smallest estimate.

The interval need not always retain a minimizer. At the first step
discarding a minimizer \(x^\ast\) in the right third, the comparison
implies \(f(u)-f(v)\leq2\nu\). Convexity gives
\[
 f(v)-f(x^\ast)
 \leq\frac{x^\ast-v}{v-u}[f(u)-f(v)]
 \leq2\nu.
\]
The left case is symmetric. If a minimizer is never discarded, the
final midpoint is within \(LD(2/3)^N\) of optimum. Selection adds
at most \(2\nu\), so the output gap is at most
\[
 \max\{2\nu,LD(2/3)^N\}+2\nu.
 \tag{3}
\]
Taking \(\nu=\epsilon/8\) and
\[
 N=\left\lceil
 \log_{3/2}(4LD/\epsilon)
 \right\rceil_+
\]
suffices with \(2N+1\) approximate values. Conditional confidence at
adaptive queries and a union bound supply simultaneous accuracy.

On a fixed-dimensional box, recursively minimize remaining coordinates
to evaluate a partial minimum. Partial minimization over a fixed convex
box preserves convexity and coordinate Lipschitz continuity. A
recursive call with witness error \(a\) and value error \(a\)
supplies a \(2a\)-accurate partial-minimum estimate. Taking
\(a=\epsilon/32\) and final interval error at most \(\epsilon/4\)
gives witness gap at most \(9a+\epsilon/4<\epsilon\).

A crude leaf-call bound, with leaf tolerance
\(\nu=\epsilon/32^p\), is
\[
 Q_p(\epsilon)\leq
 \left[
 2\left\lceil
 \log_{3/2}\left(
 \frac{4LD_\infty32^p}{\epsilon}\right)
 \right\rceil_++1
 \right]^p.
 \tag{4}
\]
The dimension dependence is poor. At fixed dimension it is
polylogarithmic in inverse accuracy. Lifted optimization adds a
threshold coordinate and uses its corresponding Lipschitz bound.
See \url{ada/exact-versus-untrusted-ranks.md},
lines 141--200 \ledger{45, 47, 49, 52}.

\section{Promised exact ranks: matching costly-query powers}

\subsection{Direct-tail value estimation}

At a fixed decision, exact-rank adaptive quadrature estimates
\[
 A_s=\int_0^1q_s(1-\alpha+\alpha v)\,dv
\]
using at most \(7k\) costly values, with
\[
 \mathbb E[Q_{s,k}\mid\text{batch}]=A_s,\qquad
 \operatorname{Var}(Q_{s,k}\mid\text{batch})
 \leq U^2/(4k^3).
\]
Levy's bounds give
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 0\leq C(x)-\mathbb EA_s&\leq c_bU/\sqrt{\alpha s},\\
 \operatorname{Var}(A_s)&\leq c_vU^2/(\alpha s).
 \end{aligned}
\]
For value tolerance \(\nu\), choose
\[
 s\asymp\frac{U^2}{\alpha\nu^2},
 \qquad k\asymp(U/\nu)^{2/3},
\]
with sufficiently large constants. Bias is at most \(\nu/4\);
Chebyshev's inequality gives constant success probability from the
variance bound. Medians of \(O(\log(Q_p/\beta))\) independent
copies make all leaf values accurate with probability at least
\(1-\beta\). The median need not be unbiased because search needs
accurate values.

In one dimension, (3) therefore gives
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 N_{\mathrm{eval}}
 &=\widetilde O((U/\epsilon)^{2/3}),\\
 N_{\mathrm{draw}}
 &=\widetilde O(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2)).
 \end{aligned}
 \tag{5}
\]
At fixed dimension, use the leaf tolerance and call count in (4).
Taking \(\beta=O(\epsilon/U)\) converts high-probability accuracy
to expected gap \(O(\epsilon)\).

Fully evaluating empirical-CVaR batches in the same search gives the
prediction-free bound
\[
 \widetilde O(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2)).
\]
The equal-quantile family matches its accuracy and rarity powers up to
logarithms under P's sampling interface.
See \url{ada/exact-versus-untrusted-ranks.md},
lines 202--260 \ledger{45, 47, 52}.

\subsection{A direct optimization lower bound immune to rank leakage}

Let \(b_1,\ldots,b_m\) be independent uniform signs. In cell
\([(j-1)/m,j/m)\), define
\[
 h_b(u)=a\left[
 u+\frac{j-1}{m}
 +\frac1m
 \mathbf1\!\left\{
 u\geq\frac{j-1+1/2-b_j/4}{m}
 \right\}\right].
\]
Use the monotone limit at the final endpoint. The linear term makes
\(h_b\) strictly increasing for every bit vector, with bit-independent
order. Its integral is
\[
 \int_0^1h_b(u)\,du
 =a+\frac{a}{4m^2}\sum_{j=1}^m b_j.
\]
Set \(a=U/3\), let \(B\) be Bernoulli with parameter \(\alpha\),
and let \(u\) be uniform and independent. Define
\[
 J_b(x;B,u)=B[2a+x(h_b(u)-a)],
 \qquad x\in[-1,1].
\]
Sample losses are affine, \(a\)-Lipschitz, and in \([0,U]\).
The positive component is separated from zero, so
\[
 C_b(x)=2a+\frac{ax}{4m^2}\sum_{j=1}^m b_j.
 \tag{6}
\]

Grant \((B,u)\) for free. At \(x>0\), positive losses are ordered
by \(u\); at \(x<0\), by reversed \(u\); at \(x=0\), tie-breaking
is bit-independent. Thus coordinates simulate every common-decision
rank response without exposing the signs. Repeated and nested ranks
supply no additional bit information.

A costly query at a nonzero decision reveals at most its cell's bit.
Grant the stronger oracle that always reveals that bit. If prior
expected costly queries are at most \(n\), the posterior mean of
\(S_m=\sum_jb_j\) is a stopped sum of exposed independent signs.
Its second moment is at most \(n\), hence its expected absolute value
is at most \(\sqrt n\). Bayes expected gap in (6) is at least
\[
 \frac a{4m^2}
 \left(\mathbb E|S_m|-\sqrt n\right).
\]
Since
\[
 \mathbb E|S_m|
 \geq
 \frac{(\mathbb ES_m^2)^{3/2}}
      {(\mathbb ES_m^4)^{1/2}}
 \geq\sqrt{m/3},
\]
taking \(m=12n\), with integer rounding, gives gap at least
\(a/(576n^{3/2})\). Therefore
\[
 N_{\mathrm{eval}}
 =\Omega((U/\epsilon)^{2/3}).
 \tag{7}
\]

This permits arbitrary coordinate probes and free draws. It is a
direct optimization lower bound, not an estimation bound multiplied
by a horizon. The common-decision restriction remains essential.

Separately, the Bernoulli-sign family requires
\(\Omega(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2))\) draws even if every sampled loss
function is revealed for free. Different subfamilies establish the
worst-case marginal resource bounds; no single instance is claimed
to maximize both.
See \url{ada/exact-versus-untrusted-ranks.md},
lines 262--354 \ledger{47, 49, 50, 52}.

\section{Untrusted ranks: the retained residual estimator}

\subsection{A fixed convex mean}

At lifted point \(z=(x,t)\), let
\[
 f(v)=\phi(z;\xi_{\pi(\lceil sv\rceil)}),
 \qquad M=U/\alpha.
\]
Its integral is the empirical mean of \(\phi\), independently of
the permutation.

For tolerance \(\nu\in(0,M]\), take \(k=\lceil M/\nu\rceil\)
equal cells and query endpoints consistently. On each cell, let \(H\)
equal the midpoint of endpoint values. Put
\[
 R=f-H,\qquad |R|\leq M,\qquad v_R=\int_0^1R(v)^2\,dv.
\]
For exact ranks, cell ranges \(r_j\) satisfy
\[
 v_R\leq\frac1{4k}\sum_jr_j^2
 \leq\frac{M^2}{4k}\leq M\nu/4.
 \tag{8}
\]
Arbitrary ranks satisfy \(v_R\leq M^2\).

With independent uniform pilot probes and
\(\ell=\log(4/\rho)\), set
\[
 m_0=\left\lceil16M\ell/\nu\right\rceil,\qquad
 \widehat v=\frac1{m_0}\sum_{j=1}^{m_0}R(V_j)^2.
\]
Since \(\operatorname{Var}(R^2)\leq M^2v_R\), Bernstein gives,
except with probability \(e^{-\ell}\),
\[
 v_R\leq2\widehat v+\frac{4M^2\ell}{m_0}
 =:\overline v.
 \tag{9}
\]
The square-root term is controlled by
\[
 \sqrt{2M^2v_R\ell/m_0}
 \leq v_R/2+M^2\ell/m_0.
\]

Choose an independent main sample with
\[
 n=\left\lceil
 64\ell\left(\frac{\overline v}{\nu^2}+\frac M\nu\right)
 \right\rceil,
\]
and return
\[
 \widehat F_s
 =\int_0^1H(v)\,dv+\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^nR(W_j).
 \tag{10}
\]
Conditional on the batch, anchors, and pilot, this has exactly the
empirical lifted mean. On (9), Bernstein bounds main error by
\(\nu/2\), except with probability \(2e^{-\ell}\). A batch size
\[
 s\geq2M^2\nu^{-2}\log(2/\rho)
 \tag{11}
\]
controls population error by another \(\nu/2\).

The main sample count is chosen from an independent pilot. One may
instead decide before main probes to evaluate the whole remaining
batch and return its exact mean. Switching after inspecting main
samples is not used.

Since \(\mathbb E\widehat v=v_R\), conditional expected costly probes
satisfy
\[
 \mathbb EN_{\mathrm{eval}}(z)
 =O\!\left[
 \left(\frac M\nu+\frac{v_R}{\nu^2}\right)
 \log(1/\rho)\right].
 \tag{12}
\]
This controls expected pilot cost without discarding failure events.
The absolute query cap is of order
\(M^2\nu^{-2}\log(1/\rho)\); a further Bernstein bound also gives
the order in (12) with high probability for this construction.

Exact ranks give \(\widetilde O(M/\nu)\) costly values; arbitrary
ranks give \(\widetilde O(M^2/\nu^2)\), without known advice
quality or a trust parameter.
See \url{ada/exact-versus-untrusted-ranks.md},
lines 356--460 \ledger{48, 49, 52}.

\subsection{The retained natural-error bound}

If the permutation sorts clipped scores with
\(|J-\widehat J|\leq\eta\), their lifted values form a
nondecreasing \(g\in[0,M]\) with
\(\|f-g\|_\infty\leq e=\eta/\alpha\). If \(r_j^g\) is its
range in a cell, then
\[
 |f(v)-H(v)|\leq r_j^g/2+2e.
\]
Consequently,
\[
 v_R\leq\frac{M^2}{2k}+8e^2
 \leq M\nu/2+\frac{8\eta^2}{\alpha^2}.
 \tag{13}
\]
Together with (12) and \(v_R\leq M^2\), this gives
\[
 \widetilde O\!\left[
 \frac{U}{\alpha\nu}
 +\min\left\{
 \frac{\eta^2}{\alpha^2\nu^2},
 \frac{U^2}{\alpha^2\nu^2}
 \right\}\right].
 \tag{14}
\]
The algorithm needs neither \(\eta\) nor numerical scores.

Applying box search to the lifted objective, with fresh batches and
conditional confidence allocation, gives costly-query power \(1\)
on exact ranks and power \(2\) on arbitrary ranks at fixed dimension
and fixed positive \(\alpha\). Draws have power \(2\).
This valid but weaker bound is improved by the appended sixth round.
See \url{ada/exact-versus-untrusted-ranks.md},
lines 462--504 \ledger{48, 50, 56}.

\section{The retained price of robustness}

\subsection{A lower bound even with exact tail membership}

Let \(x\in[-1,1]\), \(a=U/4\), \(A=U/2\), and partition a
uniform coordinate \(u\) into \(N\) equal bins. Put
\(\gamma=a/(2N)\), independently of
\(B\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(\alpha)\).

On the good instance,
\[
 J_0(x;B,u)=B(A-\gamma x),\qquad
 C_0(x)=A-\gamma x.
\]
All positive losses tie, so ordering them by \(u\) is exact advice.
Grant \((B,u)\) for free. Alternative \(j\) changes only bin \(j\):
\[
 J_j(x;B,u)
 =B\left[A+x\left(-\gamma+
 a\mathbf1\{u\text{ in bin }j\}\right)\right].
\]
Then \(C_j(x)=A+\gamma x\). Losses remain affine and bounded;
the positive component remains positive. The same permutations are
returned on every instance.

Expected gap at most \(\epsilon=\gamma/4=a/(8N)\) implies
good-instance expected output at least \(3/4\), and alternative
expected output at most \(-3/4\). Couple the runs until a query
hits the altered positive-component bin. If good-instance expected
costly queries are \(n_0\), a uniform hidden bin is visited with
probability at most \(n_0/N\), even allowing coordinate probes.
Thus
\[
 \frac32\leq\frac{2n_0}{N},
 \qquad
 n_0\geq\frac{3N}{4}
 =\frac{3a}{32\epsilon}
 =\Omega(U/\epsilon).
 \tag{15}
\]
This is a lower bound on a perfect-rank instance for an algorithm
also correct under arbitrary ranks. It allows random stopping and
free certified tail membership on every instance
\ledger{46, 48, 52}.

\subsection{Worst-case arbitrary ranks and the fixed-rarity table}

With constant uninformative advice, the Bernoulli-sign family requires
\(\Omega(U^2/\epsilon^2)\) costly evaluations at fixed \(\alpha\),
even granting tail membership. Every new paid tail sign supplies
only \(O((\epsilon/U)^2)\) divergence; reevaluation supplies no
new independent sign.
See \url{ada/exact-versus-untrusted-ranks.md},
lines 506--569 \ledger{48, 50}.

For fixed \(U,L,\alpha>0\) and fixed-dimensional boxes, the retained
table is
\[
 \begin{array}{c|c|c}
 \text{Correctness and measured class}
 &\text{Upper}&\text{Lower}\\ \hline
 \text{Exact-rank promise}
 &\widetilde O(\epsilon^{-2/3})
 &\Omega(\epsilon^{-2/3})\\
 \text{Robust; exact ranks}
 &\widetilde O(\epsilon^{-1})
 &\Omega(\epsilon^{-1})\\
 \text{Robust; arbitrary ranks}
 &\widetilde O(\epsilon^{-2})
 &\Omega(\epsilon^{-2})
 \end{array}
\]
The robust upper rows belong to the same algorithm; lower bounds
already hold in one dimension. The promise row does not imply
robustness. Logarithmic gaps prevent promotion to a fully matched E1
record under Q's strict rule at
\url{inputs/Q.tex}, lines 203--208
\ledger{47, 48, 50, 52}.

\section{Retained fifth-round costs and roughness qualification}

The old robust method pays
\[
 \text{cost}=(c_0+c_p)s+c_eN_{\mathrm{eval}},
\]
where \(c_p\) includes prediction and amortized sorting.
Its fair lifted value-search comparator pays \((c_0+c_e)s\).
Skipping ranks when their known cost exceeds a fixed multiple of
the comparator preserves that declared baseline order, but promises
no rank saving on the skipped branch.

The old counts were
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 N_{\mathrm{draw}}^{\mathrm{exact}}
 &=\widetilde O(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2)),\\
 N_{\mathrm{draw}}^{\mathrm{robust}}
 &=\widetilde O(U^2/(\alpha^2\epsilon^2)).
 \end{aligned}
\]
They remain valid upper bounds, but the robust one is superseded below.
Fixed positive draw cost leaves total-cost accuracy power \(2\)
\ledger{47, 48, 50, 56}.

Approximate ranks can exhaust the earlier adaptive split cap.
If \(f=g+e_f\), \(g\) is monotone in \([0,M]\), and
\(\|e_f\|_\infty\leq e\), every final cell still has width at most
\(1/k\). Therefore
\[
 \operatorname{Var}(Q\mid\text{batch})
 \leq\frac{M^2}{2k^2}+\frac{2e^2}{k},
\]
using
\(\operatorname{Var}(g+e_f)\leq
2\operatorname{Var}(g)+2\operatorname{Var}(e_f)\).
For loss-score error \(\eta\),
\[
 \chi\leq
 \min\left\{1,\frac2k+8(\eta/U)^2\right\}.
\]
Exact ranks separately retain \(k^{-2}\). The fixed-partition
residual method avoids the split-cap issue.
See \url{ada/exact-versus-untrusted-ranks.md},
lines 571--621 \ledger{49, 50}.

\section{Appended sixth round: tail localization and random anchors}

\subsection{Scope and comparison requested by review 52}

The extension concerns one-dimensional convex stochastic losses in
\([0,U]\), with uniform Lipschitz continuity and
\(0<\alpha\leq1/2\). It uses the same common-decision rank interface.
Permutations are frozen before random anchors and residual probes.
Visible scenario features may be absent or uninformative; numerical
surrogate values are unnecessary.

The claims are expected costly-query bounds for an expected objective
gap at most \(\epsilon\), obtained first through high-probability
accuracy. Geometry enters logarithmically through interval search.
Lower families have Lipschitz constants of order \(U\).
The account does not claim matching dependence on an independently
varying smaller Lipschitz constant
\ledger{56--58}.

Interleaving the old robust algorithm's paid queries with fully evaluated
direct-CVaR search caps costly queries at
\(\widetilde O(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2))\), up to a constant factor,
by returning the first completed valid run. This verifies one route
suggested by review 52, but does not repair the old draw count.
The localized construction improves both resources and makes that race
unnecessary \ledger{54, 56}.

The full derivation is
\url{ada/tail-localized-rank-frontier.md},
lines 1--104 and 339--385.

\subsection{Localization including atoms}

Fix \(x\), write \(Y=J(x,\xi)\), and define
\[
 q_\alpha=\inf\{t:F_Y(t)\geq1-\alpha\},
 \qquad
 F_x(t)=t+\alpha^{-1}\mathbb E(Y-t)_+.
\]
The quantile is a minimizing threshold.

For \(\alpha>1/8\), take \(t_0=0\).
Otherwise fully evaluate an independent localization batch of size
\[
 n_0\geq c\alpha^{-1}\log(2/\zeta),
\]
and take its \(k_0\)-th largest value, where
\(k_0=\lceil2\alpha n_0\rceil\).
With probability at least \(1-\zeta\),
\[
 t_0\leq q_\alpha,\qquad \Pr(Y>t_0)\leq8\alpha.
 \tag{16}
\]

For the small-\(\alpha\) branch, \(t_0>q_\alpha\) requires at least
\(k_0\) observations strictly above \(q_\alpha\), each with probability
at most \(\alpha\). A binomial upper-tail bound gives failure at
most \(\exp(-\alpha n_0/3)\). If
\(\Pr(Y>t_0)>4\alpha\), then \(t_0<q_{4\alpha}\), so fewer than
\(k_0\) observations are at least \(q_{4\alpha}\), each with
probability at least \(4\alpha\). A binomial lower-tail bound gives
failure at most \(\exp(-\alpha n_0/2)\), with sufficiently large
constants handling rounding.

Strict and non-strict inequalities matter: atoms at the threshold
contribute zero to the hinge. No density assumption or quantile
uniqueness is used. In the large-\(\alpha\) branch,
\(\Pr(Y>0)\leq1\leq8\alpha\) deterministically.

On (16), minimizing \(F_x\) over \([t_0,U]\) preserves CVaR, and
every queried threshold in that interval has hinge support probability
at most \(8\alpha\). This is a separate inner problem at each \(x\).
No convexity claim is made for a joint decision-dependent domain
\(\{(x,t):t\geq t_0(x)\}\).
See \url{ada/tail-localized-rank-frontier.md},
lines 106--159 \ledger{55, 56}.

\subsection{Random anchors preserve the sparse-support gain}

For \(t\in[t_0,U]\), draw a fresh batch of size \(s\) and receive
its predicted permutation \(\pi\). Define the hinge step function
\[
 f(v)=\frac{
 (J(x,\xi_{\pi(\lceil sv\rceil)})-t)_+}{\alpha},
 \qquad M=U/\alpha.
\]
The integral is the empirical mean hinge for every permutation.
The deterministic threshold \(t\) is added back at the end.

Partition \((0,1]\) into \(k\) equal cells. After ranks are frozen,
choose an independent uniform anchor \(A_j\) in each cell \(I_j\),
and set \(H(v)=f(A_j)\) there. For
\(R=f-H\) and \(v_R=\int R^2\),
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E_Av_R
 &=\frac1k\sum_j
 \mathbb E_{V,W\sim\mathrm{Unif}(I_j)}
 (f(V)-f(W))^2\\
 &=\frac2k\sum_j\operatorname{Var}_{I_j}(f).
 \end{aligned}
 \tag{17}
\]
Hence, with empirical strict-exceedance frequency
\[
 p_s=\frac1s\sum_{l=1}^s
 \mathbf1\{J(x,\xi_l)>t\},
\]
arbitrary ranks satisfy
\[
 \mathbb E_Av_R\leq2\int f^2\leq2M^2p_s.
\]
On successful localization,
\[
 \mathbb E_{\mathrm{batch},A}v_R\leq16U^2/\alpha.
\]

Suppose scores inducing the order satisfy
\(|J-\widehat J|\leq\eta\).
After clipping, their hinge is monotone \(g\in[0,M]\), with
\(\|f-g\|_\infty\leq e=\eta/\alpha\).
Let \(r_j\) be its essential cell range; then \(\sum_jr_j\leq M\).
For points in a cell,
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 (f(v)-f(w))^2
 \leq{}&(2r_j^2+8e^2)\\
 &\times
 \mathbf1\{f(v)>0\text{ or }f(w)>0\}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The indicator is legitimate because the left side vanishes if both
hinges vanish. Averaging gives
\[
 \mathbb E_Av_R
 \leq\frac{2M^2}{k}+16e^2p_s.
\]
Thus, on (16),
\[
 \mathbb E_{\mathrm{batch},A}v_R
 \leq
 \min\left\{
 \frac{2M^2}{k}+\frac{128\eta^2}{\alpha},
 \frac{16U^2}{\alpha}
 \right\}.
 \tag{18}
\]
Exact ranks also give the stronger pathwise statement
\(v_R\leq M^2/k\).

Random anchors are essential to this improvement. Under wrong ranks,
fixed endpoints can all contain rare positive hinges while almost all
other positions have zero hinge, spreading the interpolant's error
across the whole rank domain. Uniform anchors prevent that deterministic
placement from controlling expected residual variance.
The proof does not assume approximately sorted hinge support.
See \url{ada/tail-localized-rank-frontier.md},
lines 161--237 \ledger{55, 56}.

\subsection{Adaptive leaf estimation and failure-event costs}

For leaf tolerance \(0<\nu\leq U\), take
\[
 k=\lceil M/\nu\rceil,\qquad \ell=\log(c/\delta).
\]
Apply the independent pilot from (9)--(10) to the anchored residual:
\[
 \begin{gathered}
 m_0=\lceil16M\ell/\nu\rceil,\qquad
 \widehat v=\frac1{m_0}\sum_{j=1}^{m_0}R(V_j)^2,\\
 \overline v=2\widehat v+4M^2\ell/m_0,\\
 n=\left\lceil
 64\ell(\overline v/\nu^2+M/\nu)
 \right\rceil.
 \end{gathered}
\]
Return
\[
 \widehat F_x(t)
 =t+\int H+\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^nR(W_j).
 \tag{19}
\]
Conditional on batch, anchors, and pilot, its expectation is exactly
the empirical lifted mean. Independent main probes give error at most
\(\nu/2\) with the stated confidence.

Cache all revealed values. If the planned main probes would cost more
than evaluating the remaining batch, decide before those probes to
return the fully evaluated batch mean instead. This preserves the
conditional mean and caps true evaluations by \(s\).

On (16), the random hinge has range at most \(M\) and second moment
at most \(8U^2/\alpha\). A batch size
\[
 s=\left\lceil
 c\left(
 \frac{U^2}{\alpha\nu^2}
 +\frac{U}{\alpha\nu}
 \right)\log(c/\delta)
 \right\rceil
 \tag{20}
\]
therefore approximates \(F_x(t)\) within another \(\nu/2\).
There is no empirical-CVaR minimization bias at this leaf.

Using \(\mathbb E\widehat v=v_R\), expected costly probes, including
anchors and pilot, are
\[
 O\!\left[
 \left(k+\frac M\nu+\frac{v_R}{\nu^2}\right)
 \log(c/\delta)\right].
\]
Averaging (18) yields
\[
 \widetilde O\!\left(
 \frac{U}{\alpha\nu}
 +\frac{\min\{\eta,U\}^2}{\alpha\nu^2}
 \right).
 \tag{21}
\]
The exact-rank specialization is
\(\widetilde O(U/(\alpha\nu))\).

Choose localization failure probability to satisfy the global
confidence allocation and \(\zeta\leq\nu/U\). The batch cap makes
its expected cost contribution at most
\(\widetilde O(U/(\alpha\nu))\), already absorbed in (21).
Localization acquisition cost
\(O(\alpha^{-1}\log(1/\zeta))\) is also absorbed.
Pilot cost is controlled by its exact expectation, not by conditioning
away pilot failure.
See \url{ada/tail-localized-rank-frontier.md},
lines 239--337 \ledger{56, 58}.

\subsection{Nested search and the resulting upper bounds}

For each outer decision, localize once and minimize the convex
\(F_x(t)\) on \([t_0,U]\) by retained-candidate search.
Its threshold Lipschitz constant is at most \(1/\alpha\).
On successful localization, the inner minimum is \(C(x)\).
The outer search therefore acts on the original convex,
\(L\)-Lipschitz CVaR over a fixed interval.

An explicit allocation is outer value tolerance \(w=\epsilon/8\)
and leaf tolerance \(\nu=w/16=\epsilon/128\).
Stop the inner interval when its Lipschitz-length bound is at most
\(w/4\). Formula (3) then gives a candidate within \(3w/8\) of
the inner minimum, whose estimated value is within \(\nu\) of its
true value. The returned estimate is within \(w\) of \(C(x)\).
An outer final Lipschitz-length bound \(\epsilon/4\) gives gap at
most \(\epsilon/2\) on the success event.

The deterministic call bounds are
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 Q_x&=O(1+\log_+(LD/\epsilon)),\\
 Q_t&=O(1+\log_+(U/(\alpha\epsilon))).
 \end{aligned}
\]
Allocate conditional failure probabilities over \(Q_xQ_t\).
With total failure at most \(\epsilon/(2U)\), bounded true gap
converts the result to expected gap at most \(\epsilon\).

The same algorithm, without knowing \(\eta\), consequently satisfies
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E N_{\mathrm{eval}}
 &=\widetilde O\!\left(
 \frac{U}{\alpha\epsilon}
 +\frac{\min\{\eta,U\}^2}{\alpha\epsilon^2}
 \right),\\
 N_{\mathrm{draw}}
 &=\widetilde O\!\left(
 \frac{U^2}{\alpha\epsilon^2}\right).
 \end{aligned}
 \tag{22}
\]
The adaptive evaluation count is an expected bound. High-probability
claims here concern objective accuracy; the unconditional cached count
is capped by all drawn batches. No corresponding pathwise
error-adaptive cost statement is claimed.

The arbitrary-rank costly-query bound now agrees with the direct
empirical-CVaR prediction-free search baseline in
\(U,\alpha,\epsilon\), up to logarithms. It no longer relies on
the weaker lifted-only comparator.
See \url{ada/tail-localized-rank-frontier.md},
lines 339--385 \ledger{56, 58}.

\section{Appended sixth round: matching lower expressions}

\subsection{Hidden promotion outside the current tail}

Set
\[
 A=U/4,\qquad A'=3U/4,\qquad c=U/8.
\]
Let \(B\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(\alpha)\). Conditional on \(B=0\),
partition a uniform coordinate into \(N\) equal bins, each of
unconditional mass \(m=(1-\alpha)/N\leq\alpha\), and put
\[
 \gamma=\frac{mc}{2\alpha-m}.
\]
The good instance is
\[
 J_0(x)=B(A-\gamma x),\qquad
 C_0(x)=A-\gamma x.
\]
Alternative \(j\) promotes only non-tail bin \(j\) from zero to
\(A'+cx\). The promoted loss lies in \([5U/8,7U/8]\), above the
old tail in \([U/8,3U/8]\). All sample losses remain affine and
bounded. Its CVaR is
\[
 C_j(x)=
 \left(1-\frac m\alpha\right)(A-\gamma x)
 +\frac m\alpha(A'+cx),
\]
with slope
\[
 -\gamma+\frac m\alpha(c+\gamma)=\gamma.
\]

Give original component labels and coordinates \((B,u)\) for free,
and return the good-instance ranks everywhere. These ranks are exact
on the good instance, but reveal no promoted-bin information on
alternatives. Original component labels are not certified true tail
membership on every alternative.

At \(\epsilon=\gamma/4\), correctness forces
\(\mathbb E_0x\geq3/4\) and \(\mathbb E_jx\leq-3/4\).
The same hidden-bin coupling as before gives
\[
 n_0\geq3N/4.
\]
Since
\[
 N=\frac{(1-\alpha)(c+\gamma)}{2\alpha\gamma}
 =\frac{(1-\alpha)(c+4\epsilon)}{8\alpha\epsilon},
\]
this proves
\[
 \mathbb E_0N_{\mathrm{eval}}
 =\Omega\!\left(\frac{U}{\alpha\epsilon}\right)
 \tag{23}
\]
for \(\alpha\leq1/2\) and sufficiently small \(\epsilon/U\).
Integer rounding can make \(\gamma\geq4\epsilon\), preserving the
argument.

The good advice can be induced by exact numerical scores, so it lies
inside every uniform-error class. The alternatives need not lie in
that class, because the algorithm must remain correct under arbitrary
advice. Formula (15) remains the separate lower bound for the stronger
interface supplying certified true tail membership on every instance.
See \url{ada/tail-localized-rank-frontier.md},
lines 387--449 \ledger{54, 57, 58}.

\subsection{Hidden tail signs with uniformly accurate scores}

For \(0<\eta\leq U\), set
\[
 b=\eta/4,\qquad a=\eta/2,\qquad A=U/4,
\]
and define
\[
 \begin{gathered}
 J_v(x)=A+B(a+bSx),\qquad
 B\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(\alpha),\\
 \Pr_v(S=1)=(1+v\gamma)/2,\qquad v\in\{-1,+1\}.
 \end{gathered}
\]
Losses lie in \([0,U]\), are affine and \(b\)-Lipschitz, and
\[
 C_v(x)=A+a+vb\gamma x.
\]
Every handle receives constant score \(\widehat J=A\), with an
independent fixed tie convention. Uniform error is at most
\(3\eta/4\). Handles carry no information about their latent \(B,S\).

Even revealing \((B,S)\) at a new paid handle gives divergence at
most \(4\alpha\gamma^2\). Adaptive selection among unqueried opaque
handles and random stopping yield total divergence at most
\[
 4\alpha\gamma^2\mathbb E_+N_{\mathrm{eval}}.
\]
Expected-gap correctness forces constant total variation separation
when \(b\gamma\geq4\epsilon\). Pinsker therefore gives
\[
 \mathbb E_+N_{\mathrm{eval}}
 =\Omega\!\left(
 \frac{\eta^2}{\alpha\epsilon^2}\right),
 \qquad \epsilon\leq c\eta.
 \tag{24}
\]
Repeated values from a handle can be simulated from its revealed pair
and give no additional independent sign. The lower bound does not
supply free tail membership or a known simulator formula that exposes
the latent bits.

Combining (23) and (24), their maximum equals their sum up to a
constant factor. When \(\eta^2\lesssim U\epsilon\), (23) dominates.
Otherwise the small-accuracy condition for (24) holds. For \(\eta\geq U\),
use the construction at \(\eta=U\). Thus, among algorithms correct
for all predictions, worst-case expected costly queries over the
uniform-error class satisfy
\[
 \Omega\!\left(
 \frac{U}{\alpha\epsilon}
 +\frac{\min\{\eta,U\}^2}{\alpha\epsilon^2}
 \right).
 \tag{25}
\]
This is a worst-case error-class statement, not instancewise optimality.
A trusted error promise restricting the correctness class would be a
different problem.
See \url{ada/tail-localized-rank-frontier.md},
lines 451--514 \ledger{57, 58}.

\section{The strengthened table and charged resources}

The promised-exact proof (5)--(7) remains unchanged. Combining it
with (22)--(25) gives the following one-dimensional table, for
\(0<\alpha\leq1/2\) and sufficiently small accuracy:
\[
 \begin{array}{c|c|c}
 \text{Class}
 &\text{Expected upper}
 &\text{Expected lower}\\ \hline
 \text{Exact promise}
 &\widetilde O((U/\epsilon)^{2/3})
 &\Omega((U/\epsilon)^{2/3})\\
 \text{Robust, exact}
 &\widetilde O(U/(\alpha\epsilon))
 &\Omega(U/(\alpha\epsilon))\\
 \text{Robust, arbitrary}
 &\widetilde O(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2))
 &\Omega(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2))
 \end{array}
\]
The robust rows belong to the same algorithm, which also attains
(22) without knowing \(\eta\). All upper rows use
\(\widetilde O(U^2/(\alpha\epsilon^2))\) draws.
The separate Bernoulli-sign draw lower bound matches that marginal
resource order up to logarithms \ledger{57, 58}.

If \(c_0,c_p,c_e\) charge draws, prediction and sorting per handle,
and true values, respectively, robust total cost is bounded by
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \widetilde O\!\Bigg[
 &(c_0+c_p)\frac{U^2}{\alpha\epsilon^2}\\
 &+c_e\left(
 \frac{U}{\alpha\epsilon}
 +\frac{\min\{\eta,U\}^2}{\alpha\epsilon^2}
 \right)\Bigg].
 \end{aligned}
\]
The fair prediction-free comparator pays
\[
 \widetilde O\!\left(
 (c_0+c_e)\frac{U^2}{\alpha\epsilon^2}\right).
\]
Sorting may introduce an additional logarithmic computational factor.
If ranks are too expensive, skipping them preserves the baseline but
promises no advice-dependent gain.

Fixed positive draw cost still leaves total-cost accuracy power \(2\).
The table characterizes information resources with explicit charging,
not an optimal monetary frontier. It has logarithmic gaps and is not
a strict E1 entry under Q's matching rule. Inefficient fixed-dimensional
box extensions are available; efficient dimension dependence,
communication guarantees, and last-iterate results are not claimed.
See \url{ada/tail-localized-rank-frontier.md},
lines 516--554 \ledger{58, 60}.

\section{Sources outside Q and P}

The sources below support the arguments and delimit novelty. Locations
refer to the pooled sources recorded in the working notes. Successful
retrievals used the shared pool; failed retrievals were not replaced
with agent-folder copies.

\begin{itemize}
 \item Levy, Carmon, Duchi, and Sidford,
 \emph{Large-Scale Methods for Distributionally Robust Optimization},
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.05893}.
 Recorded source locations include 2116--2196 for expected-minibatch
 bias, 2556--2675 for multilevel estimation, 2712--2738 for complexity,
 2771--2846 for lower bounds, and 5456--5505 for objective variance.
 Expected-minibatch analysis and true-rank multilevel estimation are
 established ingredients \ledger{4, 7, 18, 30, 47}.

 \item \emph{QuEst: Enhancing Estimates of Quantile-Based
 Distributional Measures Using Model Predictions},
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.05220}.
 Source lines 593--691 contain the estimator and asymptotic theorem
 motivating the finite-batch convexity check \ledger{6, 8}.

 \item Zrnic and Candes, \emph{Active Statistical Inference},
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.03208},
 already supplies augmented inverse-propensity correction and convex
 M-estimation; recorded locations are 322--345 and 434--460.
 The correction identity is not new here \ledger{17, 59}.

 \item \emph{Cost-Optimal Active AI Model Evaluation},
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.07949},
 separates cheap and expensive resources and optimizes cost--variance;
 recorded locations are 340--457 and 733--785.
 \emph{Robust Sampling for Active Statistical Inference},
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08991},
 studies interpolation toward uniform sampling, with the inspected
 comparison asymptotic under consistent tuning
 \ledger{17, 19}.

 \item Chaudhuri, Peherstorfer, and Willcox,
 \emph{Multifidelity Cross-Entropy Estimation of Conditional
 Value-at-Risk for Risk-Averse Design Optimization},
 \url{https://doi.org/10.2514/6.2020-2129}.
 Its abstract establishes earlier multifidelity CVaR design.
 Pool retrieval returned HTTP 429; detailed convergence statements
 were not inspected \ledger{17}.

 \item Curi et al.,
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.12511},
 identify rejected non-tail samples and ideal-tail sampling as central
 issues, at recorded lines 279--285 and 478--518.
 The inspected formulations do not use the same charged rank interface.

 \item Classical antecedents include Neyman's double sampling,
 \url{https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1938.10503378},
 Rao's related work,
 \url{https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/60.1.125},
 and Hesterberg's defensive mixtures,
 \url{https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1995.10484303}.
 Publisher records or abstracts were checked, but pool failures
 prevented detailed theorem inspection. Adaptive stratification,
 \url{https://jmlr.org/papers/v16/carpentier15a.html},
 and the retrieved source
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.2892},
 lines 682--733, provide antecedents for learning oracle allocations
 \ledger{22, 30}.

 \item Pieraccini and Vanzan,
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.10084},
 are a close CVaR screening antecedent. Source lines 94--103 use a
 reduced model to estimate moving quantiles and screen evaluations;
 lines 283--327 require a computable pointwise error bound containing
 the true risk region. Arbitrary wrong ranks distinguish the present
 assumptions but do not establish novelty \ledger{30, 40, 59}.

 \item Ganesh and Nobile,
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03485},
 combine multilevel simulation with CVaR optimization through a PDE
 discretization hierarchy. Hu et al.,
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.11084},
 give a general nonasymptotic multilevel bias--variance--cost framework.
 The retained optimization recursion is not an advance over that
 framework \ledger{30}.

 \item The third-round audit also records adaptive CVaR sampling in
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11515}
 and charged pilot allocation with a defensive anchor for fixed-policy
 evaluation in
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38096}.
 These limit generic claims about moving-tail and pilot sampling
 \ledger{30}.

 \item Novak, \emph{Quadrature formulas for monotone functions},
 \url{https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9939-1992-1086337-X},
 is the classical adaptive randomized integration antecedent.
 Direct pool retrieval failed with HTTP 429.
 Gaillard, Gerchinovitz, and de Montbrun,
 \emph{Adaptive approximation of monotone functions},
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.07530},
 explicitly record the rate distinction at lines 335--354.
 Lines 648--746 develop adaptive quadrature, including the conditional
 mean and area-squared variance argument at 695--729
 \ledger{37, 40, 59}.

 \item \emph{Integration of bounded monotone functions: Revisiting the
 nonsequential case, with a focus on unbiased Monte Carlo
 (randomized) methods},
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.00423},
 gives the exact nonadaptive equal-width variance \(1/(4k^2)\),
 at recorded source lines 387--423 \ledger{40}.

 \item Dell and Clutter,
 \emph{Ranked Set Sampling Theory with Order Statistics Background},
 \url{https://doi.org/10.2307/2556166},
 is a close lead for cheap ranks, expensive measurements, and imperfect
 rankings. The review
 \url{https://doi.org/10.5402/2012/568385}
 identifies it, but the primary detailed theorem remained inaccessible.
 The Takahasi--Wakimoto predecessor,
 \url{https://www.ism.ac.jp/editsec/aism/pdf/020_1_0001.pdf},
 also failed through the pool with HTTP 429. Limited primary indexed
 text discusses order-statistic stratification, unbiased means, and
 Neyman allocation. These do not constitute a complete theorem audit.
 Arbitrary-permutation unbiasedness is not claimed as a new principle
 \ledger{40, 59}.

 \item Agarwal et al.,
 \emph{Stochastic Convex Optimization with Bandit Feedback},
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.1744},
 specifies the convex, Lipschitz, centered sub-Gaussian model at source
 lines 169--195 and the one-dimensional result at 541--568.
 Belloni et al.,
 \emph{Escaping the Local Minima via Simulated Annealing},
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07242},
 gives the approximate-value and noisy-value results at 695--749.
 Both were read from pooled LaTeX \ledger{44, 49}.

 \item Dagum, Karp, Luby, and Ross,
 \url{https://doi.org/10.1137/S0097539797315306},
 and Mnih, Szepesvari, and Audibert,
 \emph{Empirical Bernstein Stopping},
 \url{https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~vmnih/docs/ebstop.pdf},
 are antecedents for variance-adaptive estimation.
 Pool retrievals failed with HTTP 429. In the sixth round, the Mnih
 primary text was readable through web access: Section 3, equation (4),
 gives variance divided by squared tolerance plus range divided by
 tolerance, with logarithms. It discusses relative error and omits
 that proof in the conference text. The independent-pilot absolute-error
 derivation here does not rely on the omitted proof
 \ledger{49, 59}.

 \item Karabag, Neary, and Topcu,
 \emph{Smooth Convex Optimization Using Sub-Zeroth-Order Oracles},
 \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.00667},
 defines comparisons of objective values at different decisions,
 at pooled source lines 213--223 and 811--818.
 That oracle differs from common-decision scenario ranks. Its theorem
 does not settle this frontier, and these lower bounds do not apply to
 its cross-decision comparator \ledger{59}.
\end{itemize}

Detailed earlier audits appear in
\url{ada/two-resource-screening.md}, lines 293--343;
\url{ada/lifted-correction-and-baselines.md},
lines 294--308 and 323--343;
\url{ada/moving-tail-rank-correction.md}, lines 545--629;
\url{ada/rank-quadrature-and-robust-lifting.md}, lines 563--605;
and \url{ada/exact-versus-untrusted-ranks.md}, lines 623--685.
The final audit and access qualifications are in
\url{ada/tail-localized-rank-frontier.md}, lines 556--637.

Searches covered CVaR screening, active inference, monotone integration,
ranked-set sampling, noisy-value and comparison-oracle optimization,
variance-adaptive estimation, and prediction-dependent lower bounds.
Failure to locate an identical theorem is not evidence of novelty
\ledger{17, 30, 40, 50, 59}.

\section{Verification and limits of the evidence}

The recorded checks supplement the arguments; they do not replace
proofs or establish practical speedups.

\begin{itemize}
 \item \url{ada/check-derivations-run1.log}, lines 1--7:
 exact CVaR and convexity checks, plus a numerical KL grid
 \ledger{9}.

 \item \url{ada/check-screening-round2-run2.log}, lines 1--7:
 10,980 screening and moment checks and 307 lifted checks, totaling
 11,287 \ledger{19}.

 \item \url{ada/check-moving-tails-round3-run1.log}, lines 1--5:
 28,632 fractional-weight rank checks and 1,331 audit-moment checks,
 plus level-cost, moving-mask, and category-recovery checks.
 Script: \url{ada/check_moving_tails.py} \ledger{31}.

 \item \url{ada/check-rank-quadrature-round4-run1.log}, lines 1--7:
 36,503 checks covering arbitrary orders, inverse-cube variance,
 fractional CVaR, larger arrays, cost certificates, and posterior
 variance. Script: \url{ada/check_rank_quadrature.py} \ledger{39}.

 \item \url{ada/check-rank-frontier-round5-run1.log}, lines 1--8:
 40,711 checks covering residual identities, natural-error and
 roughness bounds, hidden-block rank invariance, Bayes constants,
 rare-bin coupling, retained-candidate search, and pilot inequalities.
 Script: \url{ada/check_rank_frontier.py} \ledger{49}.

 \item \url{ada/check-tail-localization-round6-run1.log}, lines 1--9:
 21,840 anchor-moment checks, 27,594 localized-error checks,
 45 atomic localization checks, 100 promoted-bin checks,
 162 error-family checks, and 36 pilot-cost checks, totaling
 49,778. It also records a sparse example where fixed anchors have
 32 times the random-anchor residual.
 Script: \url{ada/check_tail_localization.py}
 \ledger{57, 58}.
\end{itemize}

Review 52 independently reported successful hand checks of the
fifth-round core and requested that the retained results survive
whatever the extension found. The sixth-round extension has its
derivations and finite checks, but no subsequent verifier review
in the supplied ledger \ledger{52, 60, 61}.

\section{What is resolved, what remains open, and the smallest next step}

The predicted-VaR wrapper remains unsupported under P's original
oracle. Exact quantiles leave tail-mean uncertainty. P's value-based
residual improvement, the QuEst transfer counterexample, costed
screening, the label and population-witness endpoints, and the
convex-target corrections remain valid in their stated interfaces.

The fifth round corrected the missing noisy-value baseline and supplied
direct optimization lower bounds for common-decision rankings.
The sixth round resolves the principal parameter gap identified in
review 52: the robust algorithm now matches the direct-CVaR baseline
in worst-case draw and costly-value powers, while adapting to uniform
score error with matching lower powers up to logarithms.
Its exact-advice auditing cost can exceed the promised-exact cost
because correctness must include undetected changes outside the
current tail \ledger{52, 56--58}.

The contribution under consideration is this explicit resource
characterization, not the invention of screening, unbiased correction,
ordered integration, or variance-adaptive estimation. Those tools
have close classical antecedents. Equivalence to a ranked-set or
stratified-integration theorem remains unresolved, particularly because
primary full texts were inaccessible. The mathematical account is
substantial; the novelty and application evidence remain thin.
Entry 60 recommends advancing to a research draft for expert review,
not announcing a novel publication \ledger{59, 60}.

Remaining technical boundaries include logarithmic optimality,
efficient dimension dependence, and richer interfaces with free
cross-decision comparisons. The lower bounds involving hidden tails
require that certified true tail membership is not supplied on every
instance. The error-class frontier requires correctness outside the
class in which cost is measured. Neither restriction can be silently
removed. No distributed prediction theorem, communication gain, or
new last-iterate theorem has been established. Adding P's network
recursion alone would not resolve these questions.

The smallest next step is a theorem-level novelty audit of
(22)--(25) in the one-dimensional opaque-handle model. Obtain and
compare the primary ranked-set and variance-adaptive inference
statements, explicitly mapping their information, correctness class,
error parameter, and charged resources. At the same time, independently
check the random-anchor sparse-support step and the promoted-bin
coupling, which are the new parts after review 52. This would decide
whether the strengthened frontier is a distinct optimization result
or a direct reformulation of established integration theory.

If practical value is the deciding criterion, the smallest separate
experiment is a costly convex simulator with measured draw, prediction,
sorting, evaluation, and validation costs. Compare exact, learned, and
degraded ranks with fully evaluated direct-CVaR search at common total
budgets. Such evidence could establish usefulness of the interface in
that application, but would not settle novelty or logarithmic
optimality.

